WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.589
Over the summer that has just ended, Sweden
and Finland's path to NATO membership took

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:05.799
important steps forward.

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:11.570
On July 5 in Brussels, the Swedish and Finnish
foreign ministers signed the Accession Protocol,

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:16.090
which must now be ratified by the national
parliaments of the 30 NATO members.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:21.140
In anticipation of signing to the protocol,
Sweden, Finland and Turkey had earlier signed

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:27.460
another agreement, in Madrid, on June 29 by
which Ankara lifted its veto on the two Scandinavian

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.720
countries' entry into NATO.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.559
Turkish President Erdogan's reservations related
mainly to two issues: Stockholm and Helsinki's

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:39.309
support for the Kurdish political-military
movement of the Kurdistan Workers' Party (PKK),

00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:44.229
an entity deemed terrorist by Ankara and in
theory also by the European Union; and the

00:00:44.229 --> 00:00:50.379
sanctions imposed on Turkey from 2019 following
its military intervention in Syria that effectively

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:53.010
put a freeze on weapons sales to Ankara.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:57.949
Meanwhile, several NATO countries, including
the U.S. and Italy, have ratified the accession

00:00:57.949 --> 00:01:01.160
protocol, but some are still missing from
the roll call.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:05.850
These include Turkey itself because, despite
the Madrid agreements, diplomacy has stalled

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:11.090
over the fate of some suspected terrorist
political refugees between Sweden and Finland

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.909
whose extradition Turkey has requested, so
far always denied.

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:19.689
At least as far as Sweden is concerned, many
of the future developments, including continued

00:01:19.689 --> 00:01:24.751
support for the PKK, will depend on the new
government that was formed based on the results

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:30.549
of the September 11 polls, which saw an exploit
by the far-right anti-immigration party.

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:34.979
But beyond Turkish resistance, the accession
of the two countries to NATO still represents

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:40.079
an unusual step in the history of the Atlantic
alliance and if completed could set a precedent

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:42.009
for similar future situations.

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:47.479
First, unlike the countries that joined the
alliance in the 1990s and 2000s, our protagonists

00:01:47.479 --> 00:01:52.280
had never been considered potential allies
despite a long and strong partnership with

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.520
NATO behind them.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.950
Think of the various military exercises in
the Baltic sea around the Swedish islands,

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:02.219
the famous BALTOPS, which we have already
mentioned in the video about Kaliningrad.

00:02:02.219 --> 00:02:06.540
No one expected such a situation, let alone
that the two Scandinavian countries would

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.390
so suddenly give up their traditional neutrality.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:14.760
Besides all, Sweden and Finland are still
two politically and institutionally stable

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.340
countries, two established and solid democracies
that haven't gone through recent internal

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:24.180
conflicts or political transition processes,
unlike all those Eastern European countries

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.850
that have joined the Alliance in recent decades.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.460
Not to mention the efficient state of their
armed forces, organized and technologically

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:35.130
advanced, ready to integrate with NATO defense
systems.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:39.750
This particularity would demonstrate the pure
strategic purpose behind the two countries'

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.330
willingness to join NATO.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:46.400
Their entry therefore is not a move designed
to secure their own domestic political structure,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.070
or to frame themselves in a broader international
arena, but only the result of a defensive

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.220
strategy against neighboring Russia.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.280
It should also be considered that neither
of the two countries belonged to the Soviet

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.231
bloc: although significant in several respects,
their accession represents no reversal of

00:03:02.231 --> 00:03:04.370
the front, no break with the past.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.620
But, precisely, a NATO enlargement in the
direction of Scandinavia would certainly mark

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.290
a change from the strategies adopted so far.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:16.610
An expansionism no longer directed eastward
toward the former Warsaw Pact territories,

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:21.910
but northward, more and more toward the Arctic,
which stands to be one of the crucial geopolitical

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.450
scenarios for the near future.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:27.500
Please write below in the comments if you
would like a video on the geopolitical relevance

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:28.610
of the Arctic.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:33.950
The Baltic Sea becomes a "NATO sea," and considering
that from now on all the Swedish islands covering

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:38.740
the waters in front of the Kaliningrad region
will to all intents and purposes be Allied

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:43.160
territories, the Russian fleet's room for
maneuver will be greatly reduced.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:47.190
But who will ever be the biggest beneficiaries
of this new NATO expansion?

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.810
Well obviously Poland and the three Baltic
republics, which have always been fearful

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.330
of possible Russian actions on the Baltic.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:58.600
As many have noted it's possible that Russian
retaliation will occur not so much on the

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:03.530
land border with Finland, hostile territory
for an invasion with armored vehicles and

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:07.840
infantry, but precisely by sea or from Belarus
to the three Baltic republics.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.450
Especially the Finnish one, moreover, is a
respectable army that can make a decisive

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:13.950
contribution to the alliance.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:19.220
Both because Finland is one of the few countries
to have maintained compulsory conscription,

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.440
ensuring an ever-ready and trained reserve,
and because of the recent purchase of sixty

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.210
four F-35 multi-role fighter jets.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:31.100
No less important is the presence in Helsinki
of the European Center of Excellence for Countering

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:37.120
Hybrid Threats, a research center wanted by
NATO and the EU that specializes in countering

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.190
hybrid threats, from cyber attacks to disinformation.

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:45.760
All these peculiarities would make the accession
process easier and shorter for Sweden and

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.370
Finland, which certainly won't have to wait
the 10 years or so that countries like Montenegro

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.090
and North Macedonia have had to wait.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:58.470
But so with the Baltic in hand and two more
friendly pawns on the chessboard, can Europe

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.320
say it's safe and secure?

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:01.430
Mmmh not yet.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:05.910
It's clear that finding the right military
arrangement of deterrence and self-defense

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:10.960
considering the territorial extent of the
two new entries will take several years.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.410
Excessive militarization of the region, however,
could be a gamble with unpredictable consequences,

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:21.560
and in addition, a new responsibility falls
on Sweden and Finland as defenders of a border

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:25.280
that is no longer just national but of the
entire alliance.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:30.130
After all, one doesn't join NATO just to benefit
from the clauses of the famous Article 5.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:35.170
Even if the maybe unjustified fear of possible
Russian offensive action underlies the historic

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:39.970
decision to renounce traditional neutrality,
Sweden and Finland will have to fulfill their

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:43.710
military obligations and spending like everyone
else.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:47.910
Enlargement has confirmed the importance of
an alliance until a few years earlier questioned

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.639
by many, from Trump to Macron, but it risks
getting stuck in the contradiction of its

00:05:52.639 --> 00:05:54.490
decision-making mechanisms.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:06:00.080
Indeed, NATO decisions, such as the entry
of new member countries, are made by unanimity,

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:05.150
so increasing the number of "parties" makes
this requirement more difficult to achieve.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:09.150
It's one thing to get the twelve founding
members to agree; it's another to find an

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:14.310
agreement among what with Sweden and Finland
would be as many as thirtytwo different voices.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:18.810
Voices that are more sensitive to certain
issues than others when it comes to defense.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:24.060
The difficult balance between Article 5 on
mutual defense and the open door policy set

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.870
by Article 10 would find confirmation in the
very case of Sweden and Finland, motivated

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:33.980
to protect themselves from their Russian neighbor
but geographically and politically distant

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:35.900
and insensitive to other borders.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:40.550
On balance, the entry of the two Scandinavian
countries into NATO would represent a military

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:44.020
enhancement with important geopolitical consequences.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:48.830
First and foremost, the shift of the Alliance's
center of gravity to the northeast could sanction

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:53.550
a final move away from cooperation policies
related to the southern and Mediterranean

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:56.080
fronts, leaving an equally hot sector uncovered.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:01.010
It is no coincidence that the new Strategic
Concept approved at the Madrid summit didn't

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:05.240
convince many experts, down to Mediterranean
countries such as Spain.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:09.810
Clearly, the recent events in Ukraine have
forced the NATO agenda to have a strongly

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:14.980
east-oriented policy, and it couldn’t have
been otherwise, but the omen advanced by several

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:20.610
sides, such as by Spanish Prime Minister Pedro
Sanchez himself, is clear and worrying: the

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:24.070
dangers move all-around, so watch out for
the south as well.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:29.200
In particular, what worries the Madrid government
is irregular immigration from Morocco to the

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:34.490
Melilla exclave, which is just one of many
impacts of the more complex and vast problem

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:38.650
of Western Sahara, claimed by Morocco with
the approval of Spain itself.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.430
Maybe a video about it will come later.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:45.090
And we can’t ignore the instabilities in
Libya and Sahel, the gradual French retreat

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:49.889
from Mali, and Russian interference in authoritarian
governments in the region.

00:07:49.889 --> 00:07:54.130
Add to this the primary role that African
gas and raw material deposits will play in

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:58.770
the immediate future, not to mention investments
by a giant like China, which also appears

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:03.960
in the new Strategic Concept along with Russia
in the guise of a global security threat.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:08.930
Beyond the Spanish demands, however, out of
the Madrid summit came a picture of NATO strongly

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:13.820
united in its intention to strengthen itself
in the east, increasing its military presence

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:18.860
on its borders with Russia by as many as two
hundred and sixty thousand troops, from forty

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:20.970
thousand to three hundred thousand.

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:25.020
At least for the time being, the allies have
also agreed on the need for the increase in

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:29.979
military spending, which will no longer be
able to consider the famous 2 percent of national

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:32.930
GDP as a cap but as a starting point.

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:37.409
So while Sweden and Finland are a historical
exception to NATO's enlargement policies,

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:42.519
it shouldn't be forgotten that their entry
has never been considered before and that

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:45.410
the decision is only the result of the two
national governments.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:50.379
NATO's attentions, however, had already for
several years been directed precisely eastward

00:08:50.379 --> 00:08:55.610
not only toward Ukraine, whose membership
talks were interrupted by the 2014 crisis,

00:08:55.610 --> 00:09:01.220
but toward countries such as Georgia and Moldova,
which are also crossed by sensitive territorial

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:02.220
issues.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:06.740
With the two new Scandinavian allies, NATO
can certainly count on a strong decisive presence

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:11.390
for the fate of the Baltic Sea, but Russian
reactions are still unpredictable and the

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:17.110
deterrent effect that Sweden and Finland hope
to achieve could generate opposite consequences.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:22.589
Then the question to ask is if it was actually
so necessary and vital for the two Scandinavian

00:09:22.589 --> 00:09:24.810
countries to break traditional neutrality.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:29.100
Finland seems to be the more motivated and
determined of the two, with Sweden still having

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:34.209
to deal with a public opinion and some political
forces strengthened after the recent elections

00:09:34.209 --> 00:09:36.439
that are still opposed to joining NATO.

00:09:36.439 --> 00:09:41.420
The fact is that, despite the discordant positions,
a general increase in the popularity of the

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.500
NATO institution seems to be hovering in the
air.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:48.920
And with it, the feeling that only NATO has
the strength to safeguard Europe from a wide

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.769
variety of global threats ends up convincing
even the most skeptical about the need to

00:09:53.769 --> 00:09:56.790
return to a net bipolar model.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:10:11.870
But will NATO experience a new season of enlargement?

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:16.850
Certainly partnerships with external actors
will be strengthened, but the example of Sweden

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:22.290
and Finland may have sparked a broader movement
and convinced other countries to "get in the

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.480
game" and align themselves with a side.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.050
Which doesn't necessarily have to match the
Atlantic one.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:28.660
Well, we have also finished our story today.

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:31.819
Thank you all for your attention and see you
in the next video.

00:10:31.819 --> 00:10:32.209
Bye!

